= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**Yes.** The pure order on the reals is a <dense linear order without endpoints> and has <quantifier elimination for dense linear orders without endpoints>. Over a finite parameter set $a_1<\cdots<a_m$, a complete one-variable type either specifies equality to one of these points or specifies one of the open intervals and exterior rays they determine.

Each such interval or ray contains a real number. Hence every complete one-variable type over a finite parameter set is realized. The successive-coordinate characterization gives <saturation> for all finite tuples. This uses finite parameter sets; it does not say that arbitrary types over a countably <infinite set> of parameters are realized.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**Yes.** As an <Abelian group>, the reals are a nontrivial torsion-free divisible group, equivalently a <vector space> over the rationals. Rational scalar multiplication is uniquely definable by equations such as $ny=mx$, so adding those operations is a definitional expansion.

In this language, <quantifier elimination for torsion-free divisible Abelian groups> reduces formulas to Boolean combinations of rational <linear equations>. Over finitely many parameters, a complete one-variable type either fixes a value in their <linear span> or excludes every value in that span. The first kind is realized in the span itself; the second is realized by any real number outside it. That span is <countable>, while the reals are <uncountable>. Thus every such type is realized and the additive real group is an <omega-saturated model>.

For finite tuples, the same argument successively supplies any required finite number of new rationally linearly independent elements. The order and multiplication of the real field are absent from this language, so they cannot impose additional type conditions.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**No.** The parameter-free <partial type>
$$
\{x>n\cdot1:n\in\mathbb N\}
$$
is finitely satisfiable in the ordered real field: choose a number larger than the finitely many bounds. By <compactness theorem> it extends to a <complete type> over the empty set. But no real number realizes it, by the <Archimedean property>. Therefore the ordered real field is not an <omega-saturated model>.

The named unit and addition make every integer bound expressible, even without extra parameters. This is why the obstruction is present in the ordered-field language and absent in the two reducts above.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Suppose $T$ is an <omega-categorical theory>. By the <Ryll-Nardzewski theorem>, every finite-arity <complete type> over the empty set is isolated. Let $p(x,a)$ be a <complete type> over a finite tuple in the unique <countable> model $M$, and take an <elementary extension> realizing it at $b$. The joint type of $(b,a)$ is isolated by a formula $\theta(x,y)$.

The <complete type> of $a$ contains $\exists x\,\theta(x,y)$, since this formula is true of $a$ in the extension. Hence it is true of $a$ in $M$, and some $b'\in M$ satisfies $\theta(b',a)$. Isolation of the joint type implies that $b'$ realizes $p$. Thus the <countable> model is an <omega-saturated model>.

**The converse fails.** The complete <successor theory> from question 4 has a <countable> model
$$
M=\mathcal N\ \sqcup\ \bigsqcup_{j\in\mathbb N}\mathbb Z_j
$$
with countably infinitely many two-sided successor components. It is omega-saturated. Over finitely many parameters, a complete one-variable type either fixes a finite successor/predecessor translate of a parameter or zero, or asks for a point in no component represented by those parameters and zero. The first kind is realized in the named component, and the second in an unused integer component. The quantifier-elimination description of <classification of models of the successor theory> shows there are no other cases.

Nevertheless this theory is not omega-categorical: $M$ and its standard model $\mathcal N$ are <countable> and not isomorphic. Thus existence of a <countable> saturated model is strictly weaker than <countable> categoricity.

